<ToolLayout title="Temperature vs Top-p — What's the Difference" description="Interactively explore what temperature does to model output, plus how it compares to top-p sampling.">
  <SamplingExplainer client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Temperature vs Top-p: What's the Difference</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Temperature</th><th>Top-p (nucleus sampling)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>What it controls</td><td>How "flat" or "peaked" the probability distribution is</td><td>Which subset of likely tokens are even considered</td></tr>
      <tr><td>How it works</td><td>Rescales token probabilities before sampling</td><td>Cuts off low-probability tokens below a cumulative threshold</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Low value effect</td><td>More deterministic, focused output</td><td>Only the most likely tokens considered — narrow but not fully deterministic</td></tr>
      <tr><td>High value effect</td><td>More random, varied output</td><td>A wider pool of tokens considered, including less likely ones</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>They solve a similar problem from different angles — temperature reshapes the whole distribution, top-p trims which part of it is eligible at all. Most guidance recommends tuning one, not both aggressively at once.</p>

  <h2>Why default temperature isn't always right for your task</h2>
  <p>A default temperature setting is a general compromise, but tasks vary hugely in what they need — code generation and data extraction usually benefit from low temperature (deterministic, focused output), while creative writing and brainstorming benefit from higher temperature (more varied, unexpected output).</p>

  <h2>How to use this explainer</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Adjust the temperature slider to see how it's described to affect output character.</li>
    <li>Match your task type to an appropriate temperature range.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using high temperature for tasks needing precision</strong> (code, data extraction, structured output), introducing unnecessary randomness where consistency matters more.</li>
    <li><strong>Using very low temperature for creative tasks</strong>, producing repetitive or overly predictable output when variety was actually desired.</li>
    <li><strong>Adjusting both temperature and top-p aggressively at the same time</strong>, which can produce unpredictable combined effects — most guidance suggests adjusting one primarily, not both simultaneously.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Choosing an appropriate temperature setting for a new AI feature</li>
    <li>Understanding why output feels "too random" or "too repetitive" for a given use case</li>
    <li>Learning the practical difference between temperature and top-p sampling</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What is the difference between temperature and top-p?</strong> Temperature rescales the probability distribution across all possible tokens, making output more or less random overall. Top-p instead restricts sampling to the smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability reaches a threshold, cutting off unlikely options entirely.</p>
  <p><strong>Should I use temperature or top-p?</strong> Most guidance suggests adjusting one at a time rather than tuning both aggressively together, since their combined effects on output can become unpredictable and hard to reason about.</p>
  <p><strong>What temperature should I use for code generation?</strong> Low temperature (often 0–0.3) is typically preferred for code and structured output, where consistency and correctness matter more than variety.</p>
</ToolLayout>
